  "helpMenuEntrySubtitle": "A short explanation in plain language",

  "helpIntroTitle": "What is PrivacyChat?",
  "helpIntroBody": "PrivacyChat is a chat app that nobody — not even us — can read along with. You don't need a phone number, email address, or password. Everything you need is right here on this screen.",

  "helpAccountIdTitle": "Your Account ID",
  "helpAccountIdBody": "The first time you opened the app, a long code was automatically created for you: your Account ID. Think of it as an address other people can use to reach you, without it revealing anything about who you are. You can find it by tapping the badge icon in the top right. Only share this code with people you actually want to talk to — for example over WhatsApp, text message, or in person.",

  "helpAddContactTitle": "Adding someone to chat with",
  "helpAddContactBody": "Tap the round + button in the bottom right. Paste or type in the Account ID the other person gave you, and optionally give them a name so you know who it is (that name stays only on your own device). Then you can start chatting right away.",

  "helpRequestsTitle": "What are \"message requests\"?",
  "helpRequestsBody": "If someone messages you first, before you've added them, it shows up at the top as a \"message request\", separate from your regular conversations. Open it and choose: Accept (start chatting normally), Decline (the request disappears), or Block (they can no longer bother you).",

  "helpSecurityTitle": "Is it actually secure?",
  "helpSecurityBody": "Yes. Every message is locked up on your own device before it's even sent, and nobody can open it along the way — not even the server. Only the recipient's device holds the key to open it again.",

  "helpLockTitle": "Locking the app itself",
  "helpLockBody": "Want to make sure nobody who picks up your unlocked phone can casually look through your conversations? Turn on a PIN under Settings → Security. You can also choose to use your fingerprint or face instead of typing that PIN every time.",

  "helpDelayTitle": "Why does a message sometimes take a few seconds to arrive?",
  "helpDelayBody": "The app checks every few seconds whether new messages are waiting for you, instead of the server actively notifying it. This is deliberate, so the server needs to know as little about you as possible. A message usually shows up within 1-2 seconds while you have the conversation open.",

  "helpLostAccessTitle": "Important: deleted the app or got a new phone?",
  "helpLostAccessBody": "There is currently no way to restore your account on another device. If you delete the app, or lose your phone, you also lose access to your conversations and your Account ID, and you (and your contacts) will need to start over. So be careful before deleting it.",

